{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "ticket_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A clean categorization of ticket type is the central grouping axis for the query about which ticket types drive workload. Values map soundly from short_description (database->database, VPN->vpn). Partially overlaps with the existing 'category' column but adds a normalized text-derived taxonomy."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_mechanism_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Provides an orthogonal axis (failure mechanism) useful for correlating with resolution effort. Values are reasonable readings (frequent disconnection->unstable_intermittent, unable to access->access_denied). Some are inferences but generally warranted; 'access_denied' for an intranet-access issue is a defensible reading."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_resource_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Grounded in the description, but largely redundant with ticket_category (email->email_server, vpn->vpn_gateway, database->database_server is nearly a 1:1 mapping), reducing its independent analytical leverage."
    },
    {
      "name": "scope_breadth",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A genuinely useful new dimension (single-user vs infra-wide) relevant to workload magnitude. However groundedness is weak: the short descriptions rarely state scope. 'Internet connection unstable' -> infrastructure_wide and most others -> individual_user appear to be confident guesses the text does not support."
    },
    {
      "name": "reassignment_indicator",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Reassignment is directly relevant to agent workload, a good axis. But the source samples provide no field indicating reassignment (no work_notes or assignment history), yet confident True/False values are produced per row, indicating fabrication."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_lag_bucket",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Bucketed resolution time is the core outcome variable for the query. However the samples show opened_at is not provided and closed_at equals sys_updated_on, so a duration cannot be computed from the shown evidence; buckets like 'within_1_week'/'within_2_weeks' appear unsupported. If opened_at (an original column) is actually available the grounding would be strong, but the evidence does not demonstrate it."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.82,
  "source_groundedness": 0.62,
  "rationale": "The added columns collectively give strong analytical leverage for the query: ticket_category and issue_mechanism_type supply grouping axes for workload, and resolution_lag_bucket is the outcome variable, while reassignment_indicator and scope_breadth relate to workload intensity. affected_resource_type is largely redundant with ticket_category. Groundedness is mixed: the categorical description-derived columns (ticket_category, affected_resource_type, issue_mechanism_type) are well supported by the source text, but scope_breadth, reassignment_indicator, and resolution_lag_bucket assert specifics the shown evidence does not support (scope and reassignment are not stated; duration is not computable from the sampled fields), indicating likely fabrication."
}
